"Displays a forecast distribution through nested probability intervals that often widen with forecast horizon.\n\nUse when: Readers need more than one uncertainty level around future estimates. The forecasting method yields calibrated distributions or defensible predictive quantiles.\n\nAvoid when: Bands are arbitrary scenarios without probabilities or the distribution is unsupported.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes forecast horizon. Nested boundaries encode named predictive quantiles or probability regions, with ordered fill intensity indicating coverage.\n\nBuild: Separate observed history from the forecast origin. Default to nested predictive quantile intervals and state their coverage levels. Draw the widest interval first, overlay narrower intervals, and label coverage levels.\n\nCheck: Check quantile ordering and nesting at every horizon. Verify the center is explicitly identified as median, mean, or mode. State conditioning assumptions and distinguish marginal horizon coverage from joint path coverage.\n\nPitfalls: Darker bands can be mistaken for extra probability rather than nested coverage. A widening fan is not inevitable; preserve the actual model intervals instead of forcing symmetry or growth. Multimodal highest-density regions may be disconnected; preserve gaps or choose another display instead of filling between outer endpoints.\n\nExample: Show synthetic weekly demand forecasts with median, 50%, 80%, and 95% prediction bands beyond the last observed week.\n\nAlternatives: Confidence band, Ensemble trajectory plot, Quantile dotplot."
